# Decision 3 — is this about the caller's own child?

The caller may be a parent, and some questions are about **their own child** ("how is my son doing?") rather than about the school in general ("when does term start?"). Say which this is, so the right records can be fetched before the answer is written.

Set `about_child` to true when the message asks about a particular child's own situation — their marks, attendance, timetable, fees, teacher, class, homework, behaviour, or report — **including** when:
  - the child is referred to only by a pronoun or a possessive pointing back at the conversation. This is the commonest form and the easiest to miss: in Arabic the possessive attaches to the end of the word rather than being written separately, so there is no separate word to notice.
  - the child is named rather than described. A personal name ANYWHERE in the message counts, whether it is a full name or a first name on its own, and whether or not the message also uses a possessive.
  - the subject is general school material but the question asks it **for** one child. The material is the school's; which part of it applies is that child's.

Set it to false for anything about the school as an institution — policies, term dates, fees in general, uniform rules, contact details — even when a child appears in the sentence. A child who is merely who relayed or prompted the question is not what the question is about; the test is whether answering requires knowing which child, not whether a child is mentioned.

Then say **how** the child was referred to, in `child_reference`:

  `son`      — a male child of the caller's ("ابني", "my son", "my boy")
  `daughter` — a female child of the caller's ("ابنتي", "بنتي", "my daughter")
  `child`    — sex not stated ("my child", "طفلي", "ولدي" where it plainly means "my child")
  `plural`   — more than one ("my kids", "اولادي", "both my children")
  `named`    — a personal name appears in the message, in full or first name alone; put it in `child_name` exactly as written. This wins over `son`/`daughter`/`context` when a name is present as well.
  `context`  — referred to only by a pronoun or possessive pointing at the conversation
  `none`     — `about_child` is false

Put a name in `child_name` only when the message itself contains one. Copy it from the message; never guess one, and never take one from the conversation, from these instructions, or from anywhere but the message being classified. Something else resolves who is meant, and it has the school's own list of children to check against. A name introduced here selects a real child.

**Copy the characters the message used.** Do not translate a name into another language, and do not transliterate it into another script — an Arabic name stays in Arabic letters even when the rest of your output is English. A name rewritten in different letters is not the name the message contained, so it is discarded and the parent is asked which child they meant, having already said so.

**Here too the mistakes are unequal, and they point the other way than in decision 1.** A false `about_child` costs one cheap lookup nobody sees. A missed one means the answer is written without knowing which child it is about — producing either a wrong answer, or a question the parent has already answered. When in doubt, say true.

## And then — what would answering it need to READ?

Only when `about_child` is true. Set `child_question_kind` to one of:

  `records`       — the child's OWN record, held by the school's records system and about nobody else: their marks, grades, results in a subject, attendance, absences, report card, their own timetable.
  `school_matter` — the school's own published material, looked up FOR this child: fee schedules, term dates, uniform rules, policies, what their year group studies. General material; this child's year decides which part of it applies.
  `both`          — the message asks for each of them, or you are not sure which it is.

This is a different question from decision 3, and the difference is the whole point of asking it. Both can be true of one message: a question can be about a child — the answer has to know their year group — while every word of the answer comes from published material and none of it from that child's record. Getting this wrong sends the question to the wrong place entirely.

The test is **where the answer's facts are written down**, not who the question is about:

  - Facts recorded about this child, and different for every child → `records`.
  - Facts the school published once, the same for every family, with this child's year or class deciding which part applies → `school_matter`.
  - The message asks for some of each → `both`.

A useful check: if two families asked the same question, would they get the same answer? The same answer means published material. A different one means a record.

**When the two are close, answer `both`.** `both` looks in every place and costs one extra lookup nobody sees. A wrong single answer looks in one place, finds nothing there, and tells the parent no information exists — which is the failure this field was added to stop.
{% endif %}
{% if tool_catalogue %}

## And — which of these would answering it need?

This deployment can look things up in several places. Put the name of every one the
answer would have to read into `needed_tools`, and leave out the rest.

{% for name, purpose in tool_catalogue.items() %}
  `{{ name }}` — {{ purpose }}
{% endfor %}

Three rules, and the third is the one that costs money when it is broken:

  * Name a place only when the answer genuinely needs what is IN it. A question with
    one part needs one; a question with two parts needs two.
  * Name every place a multi-part question needs, in one answer. They are looked up at
    the same time, so naming both costs no more than naming one — and naming only half
    of a two-part question means the other half goes unanswered.
  * **When you are not sure, name nothing at all.** An empty list is not a failure and
    it is not "none of them": it means you are not deciding, and everything stays
    available so the answering agent chooses for itself. That is the same outcome this
    assistant had before you were asked, so it is always safe. A confident wrong list
    is not — it takes the right place to look off the table entirely.
{% endif %}
